---
title: File Database
order: 7
description: -F, -Fl, -Fo, pkgfile
tags: [pacman, files, pkgfile, search, owns]
---

Finding which package provides a specific file, even for packages not
currently installed. Requires the file database to be synced.

@code bash
# ---- pacman file database (-F) ----

# sync the file database (must do before first use)
pacman -Fy                                # download file lists
pacman -Fyy                               # force refresh

# search for a file across all packages
pacman -F filename                        # find packages containing filename
pacman -F /usr/bin/vim                    # find package that provides vim
pacman -Fx 'bin/gcc$'                     # regex search

# list files in a remote package (not installed)
pacman -Fl package                        # list all files
pacman -Flq package                       # files only (no package prefix)

# find which package owns a file (remote packages)
pacman -Fo /usr/bin/curl                  # which remote package provides this

@text
The difference between -Qo and -Fo:
- `pacman -Qo /usr/bin/curl` searches locally installed packages only
- `pacman -Fo /usr/bin/curl` searches the remote file database (all repos)

Use -Fo when you need to find which package to install for a missing command.

@code bash
# ---- pkgfile: faster alternative ----

# install
pacman -S pkgfile

# update the file database
pkgfile --update

# search for a file
pkgfile curl                              # which package provides 'curl'
pkgfile -v curl                           # verbose (show version, repo)
pkgfile -b curl                           # search in bin directories only
pkgfile -d curl                           # search description too

# list files in a package
pkgfile -l package                        # list all files
pkgfile -lb package                       # list binaries only

@code bash
# ---- command-not-found integration ----

# pkgfile can suggest packages when a command is not found
# add to ~/.bashrc:

source /usr/share/doc/pkgfile/command-not-found.bash

# now when you type a missing command:
# $ htop
# htop may be found in the following packages:
#   extra/htop 3.3.0-1    /usr/bin/htop

@code bash
# ---- practical examples ----

# "I need to compile C code, which package has gcc?"
pacman -F gcc
pkgfile -b gcc

# "I need libssl.so, which package provides it?"
pacman -F libssl.so

# "What files does the 'openssh' package install?"
pacman -Fl openssh | grep bin/            # find its executables
pacman -Ql openssh                        # if already installed (local query)
